WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.980
On January 3rd, the United States launched
a drone strike near the Baghdad International

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.040
airport in Iraq.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.419
It killed several Iraqi and Iranian military
officials, including Iran’s top commander

00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:17.759
named Qassem Soleimani.

00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:22.920
Moments later, Iran's Supreme leader declared
Soleimani a martyr and threatened “severe

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.749
revenge” against the US.

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:27.700
Over the next few days, hundreds of thousands

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.060
of Iranians came out to mourn Soleimani's death.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:38.340
But Iran wasn’t the only place where people
took to the streets...

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.890
There were demonstrations in

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:40.890
Iraq.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:41.890
Syria.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:42.890
Lebanon.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:43.890
And Yemen.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.110
These are some of the countries where Soleimani
commanded a network of powerful militias;

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:52.120
which gave him and Iran extraordinary influence
across the region.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.289
This network made him one of the most important
people in Iran.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:59.050
It’s also what got him killed.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.090
So how did Soleimani expand Iran's influence?

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:04.080
And what happens to these militias after his death?

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.970
It all began with Iran’s Islamic revolution.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:21.940
In 1979, a cleric named Ayatollah Khomeini
led a popular movement that toppled Iran’s

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.900
monarch and established the Islamic Republic
of Iran.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.540
This new regime wanted to export their revolution
and that threatened countries all over the

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.750
Middle East.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.860
Iran was also the first Shia government that
billed itself as the preeminent leader of

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:37.940
the Muslim world.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.510
That especially threatened Iran's sunni-dominated
neighbors

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.160
The first one to act was Iraq.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.460
In 1980, dictator Saddam Hussein sent his
army to invade Iran.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.670
Other countries that felt threatened by Iran supported him.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:03.200
The US sent some weapons to Iran, but mainly supported Iraq throughout the war, hoping

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.030
to keep Iran's ambitions in check.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:11.540
The war carried on for 8 years and nearly
a million died.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:15.060
During that time, Iran was devastated and
surrounded by enemies.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:21.400
So, it devised a strategy to spread its ideology
and fight its enemies covertly at the same

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.569
time.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:27.019
But first it needed a security force to find
groups to partner with outside of Iran's

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:28.019
borders.

00:02:28.019 --> 00:02:32.900
So in the 80s, it put together an elite unit
of soldiers and spies, called the Quds Force.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:37.959
They became a part of Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary
Guard Corps, a branch of the military that

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.640
answered directly to Iran’s Supreme Leader.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.340
Next, it needed an opportunity to unleash
this force … and it found one in Lebanon.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.340
In the 1970s a civil war was raging in Lebanon.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.100
The US had sent troops as peacekeepers but
violence was spilling over into Israel.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.520
So in 1982, Israel invaded Lebanon.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.930
Several Lebanese militias fought back.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.060
Some of these militias were led by Shia clerics,
who had ideological ties to Iran.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.940
So Iran sent forces, millions of dollars,
and tons of weapons to back their fight.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:29.540
They eventually merged into one powerful Shia militia called Hezbollah.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:37.600
They attacked Israeli soldiers in Lebanon
and launched rockets over the border into Israel.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.319
Hezbollah even bombed the US embassy and barracks
killing 304 people.

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:46.170
Eventually, Hezbollah succeeded.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:52.159
The US troops left Lebanon in 1984 and Israel
pulled out in 2000.

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:53.930
Iran's dual strategy had worked.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.779
It turned Hezbollah into a reliable proxy
that could fight Israel and even the US on

00:03:58.779 --> 00:04:03.560
its behalf, without inciting conflict on
its own borders.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.669
Iran had also found an effective way to export its ideology in Lebanon.

00:04:07.669 --> 00:04:12.209
So Iran’s Quds Force started supporting
proxy militias in Palestine and Iraq.

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:18.240
As it built the foundation for a network,
a charismatic soldier worked his way up the ranks...

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.440
In 1998, Qassem Soleimani took command of
the Quds Force and within a few years he had

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.060
an opportunity to firmly establish Iran’s
influence in Iraq.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:38.400
In 2003, the US invaded and toppled Saddam
Hussein and his Sunni-dominated regime.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:43.730
This created a power vacuum in Iraq which
was quickly filled by Shias.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:48.630
Solemani used this opportunity to continue
to back Shia militias here; growing his network

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:53.100
into a powerful force that fought against
the US and other Iraqis.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.360
It became one of the most violent periods
in Iraq’s troubled history.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.490
Thousands of civilians died, many at the hands
of Soleimani’s Iraqi militias.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:08.750
But eventually, a Shia-dominated government
took control of Iraq.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.850
Soleimani had managed to solidify Iran’s
influence in Iraq when another opportunity

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.660
arose, this time, in Syria.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.760
in Syria
In 2011, protests in Syria turned into a civil

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:27.160
war, which threatened to overthrow dictator
Bashar al-Assad.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.690
Suleimani orchestrated a network of proxies
to work together to defend Assad.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:36.870
He called in Hezbollah fighters from Lebanon,
Shia-militias from Iraq, and even soldiers

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.870
from Iran.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.800
He also created two new militias with Afghan
and Pakistani fighters.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.500
All these groups fought alongside the Syrian
Army to keep Assad in power.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:53.390
This intensified a war that eventually killed
more than 500,000 people, mostly civilians

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:56.680
and displaced more than 11 million.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.810
But Assad survived.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:02.910
Soleimani was successfully exploiting conflicts
to advance Iran’s interests across the region.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:07.000
And it was making him a very popular figure in Iran.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.940
He became arguably the second most important
person in the country.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.460
More conflicts gave Iran more opportunities.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:23.970
When ISIS sparked another war in Iraq, Suleimani
again called on his network to defend Iraq

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.180
and keep ISIS away from Iran.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:30.750
By now, he had unprecedented influence and
continued to command the Shia militias in

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:35.040
Iraq directly even after they were officially
folded into Iraq's military.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.530
When a civil war erupted in Yemen, Iran threw
its support behind a rebel group.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:45.220
Now instead of being surrounded by enemies,
Iran had them surrounded.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.530
Suleimani empowered a vast array of militias
across the Middle East...

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:53.940
Many of them are excessively violent, and
have killed thousands.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:07:03.000
Many are designated terrorist organizations
by the US and EU.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.500
And many are corrupt.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.960
Lebanese Hezbollah and the Iraqi shia militias,
for instance, are the targets of mass protests

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.560
in those countries.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.730
And they’re putting down the protests with
more violence.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:18.000
But to Iran and its supporters, Suleimani’s
a hero.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:23.960
He helped build a web of militias that not
only keeps Iran’s enemies in check…

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:29.090
But also provides a pipeline for the Islamic
ideology that Iran wanted to see in the far

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.700
corners of the Middle East.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:36.400
It’s why Iran’s Supreme Leader immediately
called Soleimani a martyr and also declared

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.870
that his efforts and path won’t be stopped
after his killing.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:49.000
Even though its commander is gone, the
network remains intact.

